# zenjxl 4K ENCODE peak-RSS measurement — 2026-08-13 (macOS arm64)
#
# Purpose: answer "how much RAM does a system need to encode a 4K JXL?" at real
# 4K. jxl-encoder's own calibration (jxl-encoder/benchmarks/
# jxl_encode_mem_threads_postfix_2026-08-01.tsv) anchors at 12/20/27/48/75/108
# MP on x86-64 Linux; 8.29 MP on arm64/macOS is measured here rather than
# interpolated (CLAUDE.md never-extrapolate-memory).
#
# Commits under test:
#   zenjxl 5a92f93 (0.3.0) -> jxl-encoder 8205c895, zenjxl-decoder 4d7860f
#   rustc 1.97.1 (8bab26f4f 2026-07-14), release profile, --features
#   encode,decode,parallel (so `with_threads(N)` engages real parallelism).
# Host: macOS 26.5.2, Apple M4 Pro (12 cores / 8 performance), 24 GiB RAM.
#   NOTE: DIFFERENT platform from the 2026-08-01 calibration (WSL2 x86-64 /
#   glibc). Absolute peaks are a second data point, not a re-fit — no constant
#   in jxl-encoder/src/heuristics.rs was changed on the strength of this run.
#
# Harness: zenjxl examples/mem_probe_encode.rs, one encode per process,
#   unmodified (it already carries the threads axis).
#
# MEASUREMENT: peak RSS is `/usr/bin/time -l` "maximum resident set size",
#   which on macOS is reported in BYTES. The probe's own pre_rss_kb/vmhwm_kb
#   columns read /proc/self/status and are therefore 0 here — use
#   peak_rss_bytes. That is the WHOLE-PROCESS high-water mark: binary floor +
#   the 24.9 MB raw input buffer + the encoder working set. (The JXL probe
#   encodes straight from the read buffer, so unlike the AVIF probe there is
#   no second packed-pixel copy.)
#
# Content: real corpus images MOSAICKED at native scale (no upscaling — an
#   upscale smooths high-frequency detail and would under-state memory).
#   photo = 6 CLIC2025 training photos; screen = gb82-sc/imac_g3 + imageflow
#   whitespace-issue + a wikimedia screenshot.
#
# Grid (48 cells, all measured, 0 failures): 3840x2160 (8.29 MP) x
#   {lossy q85, lossless} x effort {3,7,9} x threads {1,8} x
#   content {photo,screen} x 2 reps.
#
# RESULTS — max / median peak RSS over content x reps:
#   mode      effort thr   max MB   med MB   max wall s
#   lossy       3     1      447      447       0.3
#   lossy       3     8      477      473       0.1
#   lossy       7     1      706      706       2.1
#   lossy       7     8      778      777       0.7
#   lossy       9     1      719      715       5.4
#   lossy       9     8      954      947       2.4
#   lossless    3     1      614      614       0.6
#   lossless    3     8      653      645       0.4
#   lossless    7     1     4773     4667      15.6
#   lossless    7     8     4600     4530      10.5
#   lossless    9     1     4830     4744      84.0
#   lossless    9     8     4847     4804      76.6
#
# Findings:
#   * Effort is the dominant axis, and it is a CLIFF, not a ramp: lossless
#     e3 -> e7 is 653 MB -> 4773 MB, a 7.3x jump at the MA tree-learning
#     band boundary. Lossy e3 -> e9 is a milder 477 -> 954 MB.
#   * Lossless e7+ at 4K needs ~4.8-5.9 GB. A 4K lossless JXL encode at
#     default-ish effort will not fit in a 4 GB container.
#   * Threading barely parallelizes lossless here: e9 t1 84.0 s vs t8 76.6 s
#     (user 79.7 s vs real 72.6 s on a spot check = ~1.1x), and the peak is
#     essentially thread-invariant. Lossy parallelizes fine (5.4 -> 2.4 s)
#     but pays ~230 MB for it at e9.
#   * Content spread (photo vs screen) is small: screen is the worst case for
#     lossless (4847 vs 4773 MB), photo for lossy (954 vs 886 MB).
#
# MODEL CHECK vs jxl_encoder::heuristics::estimate_encode_threaded
# (RGB8, 3840x2160, has_alpha=false):
#   mode/effort  thr   model min/typ/max MB    measured max MB   measured/TYP
#   lossy    3    1       612 /  707 / 1213          447            0.63  safe
#   lossy    7    1       981 / 1142 / 1996          706            0.62  safe
#   lossy    9    8       998 / 1158 / 2013          954            0.82  safe
#   lossless 3    1       658 /  767 / 1350          614            0.80  safe
#   lossless 7    1      3670 / 4311 / 7728         4772.8          1.11  OVER
#   lossless 9    8      3670 / 4311 / 7728         4847.2          1.12  OVER
#   => The lossless e7-e9 band's TYP tier UNDER-predicts measured peak on this
#      host by 11-12%. heuristics.rs documents the invariant "TYP >= measured
#      worst case + 10% margin"; that invariant holds on the x86-64/glibc
#      corpus it was fit on but does NOT hold here. The MAX tier still covers
#      the measurement with a 1.59x margin, so "will it fit" decisions that
#      read peak_memory_bytes_max remain correct — only the p50 capacity-
#      planning tier is optimistic for lossless e>=7. Not re-fit here: one
#      platform + one 8.29 MP point is not enough to move a constant that was
#      fit across 12-108 MP. Flagged for a cross-platform re-check.
#
# Columns: codec backend subsampling content w h pixels mode effort quality
#          threads rep out_bytes encode_ms peak_rss_bytes wall_s
#   encode_ms is the encode() call only (file read + startup excluded);
#   wall_s is the whole process.
